# Mutation cases for the pin under `docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md` §3.1 / §3.2 —
# the two rows that stated refusals the producer does not make.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/an_exclusion_is_measured_against_the_producer.cases`.
#
# The test this measures asserts ACCEPTANCE, and an acceptance assertion is
# the easy kind to write vacuously: a document that parses and generates
# passes it whether or not the assertion reaches anything. So each case below
# takes away the producer behaviour the corresponding sentence now describes,
# and the round is only evidence if the matching test turns red.
#
# The third case is the other half of the same defect. The prose is compiled
# into the test by `include_str!`, so a section that goes back to stating an
# exclusion is a mutation in exactly the sense this harness means — the
# binary changes, and the test either notices or is decoration.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test an_exclusion_is_measured_against_the_producer
mutation_targets sce-build/src/parser.rs docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md

mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/an_exclusion_is_measured_against_the_producer.rs

# ── The default initial state stops being resolved ──────────────────
#
# What §3.2 described before it was corrected: with the root's `initial` left
# empty, `can_generate_static` reaches the `DynamicFeatures` branch and the
# document really is refused. This is the one state of the tree in which the
# old sentence was true, which is what makes it the right mutation — a test
# that passes here is reading nothing.

mutation_case "the parser leaves the root initial state unresolved" <<'PY'
# The walk stays, and only the assignment goes. Two earlier spellings of
# this case did not compile and so measured nothing: deleting the loop left
# `mut` unused, and deleting it entirely left `is_scxml_state_element` with
# no caller. This crate denies warnings, so a mutation has to remove the
# BEHAVIOUR while leaving the shape the compiler checks.
edit("sce-build/src/parser.rs",
     """        let mut initial = root.attribute("initial").unwrap_or("").to_string();
        if initial.is_empty() {
            // Default to first child state in document order
            for child in root.children() {
                if child.is_element() && is_scxml_state_element(&child) {
                    if let Some(id) = child.attribute("id") {
                        initial = id.to_string();
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
        }""",
     """        let initial = root.attribute("initial").unwrap_or("").to_string();
        if initial.is_empty() {
            // Default to first child state in document order
            for child in root.children() {
                if child.is_element() && is_scxml_state_element(&child) {
                    if child.attribute("id").is_some() {
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
        }""")
PY

# ── The default resolves, to the wrong state ────────────────────────
#
# The nearer miss, and the reason the test asserts WHICH state rather than
# just acceptance: §scxml-3.2 and §scxml-3.3 both say "first child state in
# document order", so a machine entered at the last one is accepted,
# generated, and wrong from its first macrostep.

mutation_case "the default initial state is the last child, not the first" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/parser.rs",
     """                    if let Some(id) = child.attribute("id") {
                        initial = id.to_string();
                        break;
                    }""",
     """                    if let Some(id) = child.attribute("id") {
                        initial = id.to_string();
                    }""")
PY

# ── The hybrid child stops being named at parse time ────────────────
#
# §2.13's residue is that the child is fixed at build time, and the name is
# where that is decided. An empty `child_name` is what a hybrid invoke that
# named nothing would carry, and `generate_hybrid_child_scxmls` skips exactly
# that case — so the stub is never written and the claim in §2.13 stops
# being true of the tree.

mutation_case "a hybrid invoke carries no child name" <<'PY'
# `idx` stays consumed for the same reason the case above keeps its binding
# honest: an unused variable is a compile error here, and a mutation that
# will not compile measures nothing.
edit("sce-build/src/parser.rs",
     """                    child_name: format!("{}_hybrid{idx}", model.name),""",
     """                    child_name: {
                        let _ = idx;
                        String::new()
                    },""")
PY

# ── The section goes back to stating an exclusion ───────────────────
#
# The failure this file exists for, reproduced: a row of §3 that tells an
# author the static path refuses a construct it generates. The sentence below
# is the one that was there, shortened; what matters is that the retraction
# marker the test reads is gone.

mutation_case "section 3.1 states the refusal again" <<'PY'
edit("docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md",
     """Nothing here is excluded any more. This section used to say that
`<invoke srcexpr="pathVar"/>` was rejected when generating AOT code""",
     """`<invoke srcexpr="pathVar"/>` is rejected when generating AOT code""")
PY

# ── The table stops answering for one backend ───────────────────────
#
# Python is the backend §2.13 records as not evaluating the expression at
# all. A section that quietly drops the backend that disagrees reads as
# consensus, which is the state the document was in before this round.
#
# ⚠ The anchor was a table row until §2.13 was rewritten to state the
# divergence in prose. It is re-aimed at the SUBJECT — the sentence that
# names Python — rather than at the shape the subject happened to take,
# because the shape is what moved and the claim is what the oracle reads.
#
# ⚠⚠ This case SURVIVED from that rewrite on (found red in CI 2026-09-23):
# the oracle then required only that §2.13 NAME all six backends, and the
# contract sentence names all six, so dropping Python's own entry changed
# nothing it read. The oracle
# (`the_retracted_rows_send_the_reader_to_what_replaced_them`) now also
# requires each diverging backend's own bold entry — the record this case
# removes — and Kotlin, the other diverging backend, has a case of its own.

mutation_case "section 2.13 drops the backend that diverges" <<'PY'
edit("docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md",
     """**Python** evaluated nothing at all""",
     """**Pythen** evaluated nothing at all""")
PY

mutation_case "section 2.13 drops the other backend that diverged" <<'PY'
edit("docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md",
     """**Kotlin** resolved the value into a document""",
     """Kotlin resolved the value into a document""")
PY
